---
title: Custom Strategies
description: Replace or extend built-in drop zone strategies, or add entirely new container modes.
---

# Custom Strategies

You can replace or extend the built-in `'sortable'` and `'target'` strategies, or add entirely new modes by implementing the `ContainerStrategy` interface.

## Built-in strategies

| Mode | Strategy class | Description |
|------|---------------|-------------|
| `'sortable'` | `SortableContainerStrategy` | Position-based drop zones with insert previews. Supports `vertical`, `horizontal`, and `grid` directions |
| `'target'` | `TargetContainerStrategy` | Single drop zone covering the whole container, no previews |

## Passing a strategy factory

The built-in strategies receive `DndState` in their constructor. Since `DndState` is created inside `DndController`, pass a **factory function** when your strategy needs it — the controller will call it with the internal state:

```ts
import {
    DndController,
    SortableContainerStrategy,
    type ContainerStrategy,
    type StrategyFactory,
    type DndState,
    type DropZone,
    type DndMode,
} from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';

class MasonryStrategy extends SortableContainerStrategy {
    override readonly mode: DndMode = 'masonry'

    override calculateDropZones(containerId: string, container: HTMLElement, session): DropZone[] {
        // your masonry-specific zone calculation
        return []
    }
}

const masonryFactory: StrategyFactory = (state) => new MasonryStrategy(state)

const controller = new DndController({
    strategies: [masonryFactory]
})
```

Then use the custom mode on a droppable:

```svelte
<DndDroppable mode="masonry" id="board">
    ...
</DndDroppable>
```

## Passing a plain instance

If your strategy doesn't need `DndState` (e.g. it only overrides `calculateDropZones` and delegates animations elsewhere), you can pass a plain instance directly:

```ts
const controller = new DndController({
    strategies: [new SimpleStrategy()]
})
```

## Overriding built-in strategies

Passing a strategy with `mode: 'sortable'` or `mode: 'target'` **overrides** the built-in behaviour for that mode globally:

```ts
const controller = new DndController({
    strategies: [new MySortableStrategy(state)] // replaces built-in sortable
})
```

## Types

```ts
interface ContainerStrategy {
    readonly mode: DndMode;
    calculateDropZones(containerId: string, container: HTMLElement, session: DragSession): DropZone[];
    getTranslations(session: DragSession, registry: ContainerRegistry): Map<string, { x: number; y: number }>;
}

type StrategyFactory = (state: DndState) => ContainerStrategy;
```
